Revision history for Proc-Spawner (EXPERIMENTAL)

0.11  2026-11-10
      - Since 0.10: Proc::ShellSpawn on Windows (WS1: paths with drives,
        UNC names and \, PATH and PATHEXT, NUL, USERPROFILE and ~user, $PPID;
        t/portable.t: 223 battery lines as on Linux, and Windows' own
        cases); the event loop in its own module with a Windows backend on
        an I/O completion port, Ctrl-C included (W5, Proc::Spawner::Loop);
        sh as Perl with this module where PATH has no sh (SH1); cat, sort,
        grep from PROC_SHELLSPAWN_BIN or Git for Windows (BIN1, a temporary
        workaround); pwd inside Perl too (IP1 extended); whose echo the
        in-process echo is (EC1); PATH's separator from $Config{path_sep};
        X37 and X38 fixed; t/spawner.t runs on Windows; Perl's Windows bugs
        drafted, with every workaround of ours listed
        (doc/perl-win32-system-bugs.md).  Checked on Linux and under Wine
        (Strawberry Perl 5.38.2), not on real Windows.  The figures of
        post4-0.04 hold (the measured files differ only in $VERSION); digest
        of the code they depend on:
        144cd2b89d7d5655b5bd2bd84a861e13eb1df9d295e6045bfc620f5be1d755ef.
      - Perl's argv bug on Windows described exactly where SH1 and the
        tests mention it: every " of an argument is dropped, and one with
        blanks is split too (checked under Wine: 'echo"$0"' arrives as
        'echo$0'); before: "blanks and quotes".
      - TODO A1 (X27) rewritten in the user's four steps (2026-11-10): what
        is done (step 1; step 2 for the codes an environment switch
        covers), what is not (step 2 for files, vars and the other codes
        inside the run; steps 3 and 4), and the natural next piece.
      - BIN1, a temporary workaround (the user's follow-up (d) to WS1:
        "Replace them from %ENV (inventing suitable fallbacks)"; marked
        temporary by the user, 2026-11-10): the directories in
        PROC_SHELLSPAWN_BIN ('none': none) are searched before PATH; unset,
        on Windows: Git for Windows' usr\bin (beside git.exe on PATH, or
        under %ProgramFiles%, %ProgramW6432%, %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs),
        else MSYS2's, else Cygwin's; Windows' own sort, find, timeout and
        expand are never taken for the POSIX ones.  t/portable.t: 5
        Windows-only lines; under Wine all pass, both primitives
        (%ProgramFiles% untested: Wine sets it afresh in every process).
      - SP1 (the user, 2026-11-10; TODO A2 closed): "strict" needs no code
        for the 142 strict samples that run echo, printf, test, [, true or
        false: since IP1 these run inside Perl on every platform
        (large-subset-environment.md, section 3).
      - t/programs.t: the shell-layer checks of PROC_SHELLSPAWN_ECHO pass
        the string to t/try.pl in a file (TRY_FILE, as t/portable.t), so
        they run on Windows too; under Wine all 18 pass (2 skipped: no
        symbolic links, no coreutils), and t/portable.t passes.
      - sh as Perl with this module (SH1; the user's follow-up (c) to WS1:
        "Should not sh -c just call Perl with our module, then?
        (Configurable)"): a command sh runs as $^X -MPARSER
        -MProc::ShellSpawn=sh -e0 -- ARGS (sh_main: -c TEXT [NAME [ARGS]],
        a file, -s or stdin; other option letters as set takes them).
        PROC_SHELLSPAWN_SH: auto (the default: when PATH has no sh),
        perl, program.  run() takes args, arg0 and opt.  Tests:
        t/programs.t (10 strings the same with dash and as Perl, apart from
        dash's line numbers), t/portable.t (4 Windows-only lines; under Wine
        all pass with win_spawn.h, through system1 the one with blanks and
        quotes in an argument is TODO: Perl's argv bug, not worked around).
      - doc/perl-win32-system-bugs.md: 4, a draft report of X38's cause in
        Perl (on Windows $^F is not honoured: Perl's close-on-exec is fcntl,
        a no-op there, and its spawn passes every inheritable handle; read
        in perl 5.38.2's doio.c and win32/win32.c, reproduced under Wine
        with a 6-line script, and its control); 5, every workaround of ours
        for these bugs (module and tests) and what to do when Perl is fixed.
      - ~user on Windows (the user's follow-up (b) to WS1: "%USERPROFILE%/
        ../user"): USERPROFILE for one's own name (USERNAME), else the
        sibling of USERPROFILE of that name if it is a directory, else as
        written (it was always as written).  t/portable.t: 3 Windows-only
        lines (one's own, ~Public, none and ~..); under Wine: pass, both
        primitives.
      - EC1 (the user, 2026-11-09; TODO A2a): whose echo the in-process echo
        is: PROC_SHELLSPAWN_ECHO=coreutils (the default, as before; it
        heeds POSIXLY_CORRECT), dash or bash (their builtins: dash 0.5.12,
        escapes always but not \x, options only a first -n; bash 5.2.21 as
        bash --posix, escapes with -e, also \E, \u, \U); neither builtin
        changes with POSIXLY_CORRECT, so with dash or bash ours keeps
        matching that shell where X27's run found the program differing
        (large-subset-environment.md, section 9).  $Proc::ShellSpawn::ECHO
        holds it; an unknown value dies at load.  The program echo (outside
        %INPROC) is not affected.  Checked against the shells on 24,000
        random calls (C and C.UTF-8, with and without POSIXLY_CORRECT).
        Tests: t/programs.t, 732 runs against t/echo-shells.txt (recorded
        from dash and bash: perl t/programs.t --record-shells) and the
        shell layer; t/dash.t runs ours with PROC_SHELLSPAWN_ECHO=dash,
        with a new battery t/batteries/echo.txt (12 strings; 11 of them
        differ from dash with the default echo).
      - UNC names with backslashes tested (the user's follow-up (e) to WS1):
        t/portable.t has 7 Windows-only lines (cd to \\.\C:\windows and
        //./C:/windows, .. stopping at the share, a relative cd and test -d,
        \x, a child started there); Wine has no server shares, its device
        names are UNC names in form.  Under Wine: pass, both primitives.
        Found by them, ❰BUG❱ (ours, post-0.10 only): \x from a UNC directory
        was taken from the process's own drive, not the share (path_in);
        pwd at the root of \\.\C: printed nothing (the server '.' taken for
        a . component, Programs.pm).  t/portable.t: PATH's separator from
        %Config.
      - t/spawner.t runs on Windows too (the user, 2026-11-09: "Sure do!"):
        the programs it starts are Perl itself (true, cat, wc, pwd and the
        simple sh -c lines replaced; the Perl code from files, since Perl's
        own spawn on Windows splits an argument with blanks and a quote);
        what only POSIX has is skipped there (27 of 52).  Under Wine all
        pass through both primitives.  Found by it: X38 (ours: through
        system1 a child inherits every inheritable handle of ours; Perl's
        pipes on Windows are such, so a child can hold the write end of its
        own input; fixed: our fds from 3 up made not inheritable around the
        call and put back, as POSIX has it, not thread-safe; the user: "do
        as on POSIX"); and on Windows another waitpid does not
        take a status from the loop (the kept process handle):
        pspawn-spawner.md, G2.
      - The W5 check's text said the shell layer is not ported to Windows;
        it was ported later the same day (doc/pspawn-shell-windows.md): the
        text now says what held when it was checked (pspawn-spawner.md,
        section 8; pspawn-loop.md; large-subset-state.md, section 7), and
        TODO has the library under Wine as work.
      - PATH's separator from $Config{path_sep} (the user's follow-up (a) to
        WS1, 2026-11-09), no longer hard-coded, in one place:
        Proc::Spawner::path_split (the user, after a detour without it: "a
        helper makes sense"), used by the search emulation, win_find and the
        shell's lookups (of commands, and of . files).  On Windows it reads
        PATH as win_spawn.h and Perl's win32.c do (WS1: a "quoted;entry"
        whole; an unclosed quote runs to the end, where the shell cut the
        entry before).  Tests: t/spawner.t (both forms, a search through two
        entries), t/windows.t (a quoted entry and an empty one).
      - The user's decisions on the shell on Windows (2026-11-09): WS1, the
        rules of doc/pspawn-shell-windows.md, section 1, confirmed; pwd
        inside Perl on every platform (IP1 extended) confirmed; follow-ups
        in TODO (PATH's separator from %Config, ~user, sh -c through Perl,
        programs from %ENV, UNC names with backslashes untested).
      - Proc::ShellSpawn on Windows (the user, 2026-11-09: "Proceed with the
        shell layer on Windows"; doc/pspawn-shell-windows.md): paths with
        drives and \, PATH with ; and PATHEXT, NUL for /dev/null, $PPID from
        NtQueryInformationProcess (__win_ppid), exit statuses decoded by
        hand (no WIFSIGNALED there), no umask, no SIGCHLD handler between
        runs, ~ from USERPROFILE, traps on signals Perl lacks there,
        Permission denied (126) for what Windows cannot run; Graph: plain
        pipes, close-on-exec by __set_cloexec, no F_GETFL.  pwd runs inside
        Perl too (IP1 extended, in TODO; as coreutils 9.4, 352 runs
        checked).  A program inside Perl writing into a closed pipe gets
        SIGPIPE's 141 on Windows too.  t/portable.t: 223 battery lines with
        their output recorded on Linux (t/batteries/portable.txt); under
        Wine all the same, through both primitives, also in a Windows-way
        gmake build.  t/try.pl: TRY_FILE (the string from a file: Perl's
        own spawn mangles arguments on Windows), and status 255 for a
        string outside the subset on every platform.
      - Findability (the user, 2026-11-09): section 7 of large-subset-state.md
        opens with a list of its parts, each with the code and day it was
        measured on; IP1 and W5 are subsections of their own.  The index
        doc/large-subset/README.md names section 7's parts, section 9 of
        large-subset-environment.md, the two .tsv records and the runner's
        options.  pspawn-spawner.md, section 8: Proc::ShellSpawn on Windows,
        not ported, with what blocks it and what passes under Wine;
        pspawn-loop.md: the library under Wine.  test-pools.md: how to rerun
        ours alone after a change, the environment varied, and through Run.
        large-subset-runreport.pl -ours-from DIR (join ours of a later run
        with dash and bash of an earlier one) and -ours-vs DIR (ours of two
        runs, sample by sample), which the IP1 and W5 checks had done by
        hand; checked to give the same results.
      - doc/pspawn-loop.md: the event loop (W5) on its own page: how it works
        on each platform (the completion port, Ctrl-C, alarm), what was
        checked (a table), the known limits; linked from doc/README.md, the
        POD of Proc::Spawner::Loop and pspawn-spawner.md, section 8.
      - W5 checked over the library (2026-11-09): on Linux, ours alone at
        main against the rebuilt table, sample by sample: no change from W5
        (55 changed, all timing, process IDs, inode numbers, or X37's fix).
        Under Wine the library cannot run: Proc::ShellSpawn is not ported
        (run() dies at getppid, then at fcntl F_GETFL in Graph; in TODO);
        the Windows build's tests pass there, and all 45,501 parse trees
        are the same as on Linux.  large-subset-state.md, section 7.
      - large-subset-runcompare.pl -env mtime fixes the worker's directory
        too (the run's ".."): joining runs of different days made ls -la
        differ.
      - Section 7 of large-subset-state.md with the in-process programs
        (IP1, the code of 0.10), rebuilt from the run of post-0.09 rather
        than run again: dash and bash unchanged; ours run again on the 1,055
        samples where it ran echo, printf, test, [, true or false itself
        (and on 300 others as a check: the same, all 300), in the same
        workers.  2 samples change class: printf "\u20AC" (X37, fixed) and a
        race.  README and TODO say so.
      - X37 (ours; 0.10): the in-process printf printed \u and \U escapes as
        UTF-8 even in the C locale, where coreutils 9.4 prints the escape
        (\u20AC, \U0001F600; also above U+10FFFF), took a surrogate (\ud800)
        where coreutils stops with "invalid universal character name" and
        status 1, and %b dropped the text before a bad escape.  Now as
        coreutils; 14 runs added to t/programs.txt.  Found by the behaviour
        run with the in-process programs.
      - large-subset-runcompare.pl: -shells (run only some of dash, bash,
        ours), -workers-from DIR (each sample in the worker, so the
        directory and user, of an earlier run), and a restart that works:
        the samples already done were never found (the records' keys are
        sorted, "id" is not first).
      - W5 (the user, 2026-11-08: "Use the Windows' way"; 2026-11-09: "in a
        separate module, with an \"elegant\" API which may be shared between
        your POSIX approach and Win32"): Proc::Spawner::Loop, the one event
        loop: read, write, child, after (a timer), defer, wait_until, poll,
        pipe; each watch can be cancelled.  Backends: POSIX (select and
        SIGCHLD, the code moved from Proc::Spawner) and Win32 (win_loop.h:
        one I/O completion port; overlapped I/O where the handle allows it,
        a helper thread where not; RegisterWaitForSingleObject for children).
        Proc::Spawner's on_read, on_write, defer, wait_until, poll remain as
        its old names; a read's callback now also gets the errno of an
        error.  On Windows, Proc::Spawner::Graph writes into a pipe directly
        (no /proc).  t/loop.t: 12 tests on Linux, 17 under Wine with
        Strawberry Perl (through both Windows primitives), also in a
        Windows-way build (perl Makefile.PL, gmake, gmake test).
      - W5 on Windows (the user, 2026-11-09: "Can a handler for Ctrl-C
        report to the loop instead?"): the wait has no time limit of its
        own; while wait_until runs, a console control handler of ours posts
        Ctrl-C (and Ctrl-Break, close, shutdown) to the completion port, and
        the wait gives Perl's own handler up to 200 ms to mark the signal;
        while an alarm is set, 50 ms slices with Perl's message check.
        Checked under Wine with a console: the %SIG{INT} handler ran 0.04 s
        after Ctrl-C (without ours: at the next event, 3 s later); alarm on
        time; default and IGNORE as before.  t/loop.t: a test of it (skipped
        without a console).
      - large-subset-runcompare.pl -only with an empty list (or an empty
        @FILE) is an error: it meant every sample.
      - large-subset-state.md, section 7, and README: the behaviour table and
        "Through Run" were run before IP1, with the coreutils programs (what
        PROC_SHELLSPAWN_INPROC=none gives now), which they now say; the re-run
        with the in-process default is in TODO (not re-measured).
      - large-subset-state.md, section 7: Run's commit is a2be65e (was
        42988a4: repository ilyaz-ai/run's history rewritten with GitHub's
        no-reply address, 2026-11-08; the same Run.pm).

0.10  2026-11-08
      - Since 0.09: Spawner.so loads with perl before 5.34 (X36, RT #182809;
        thanks to Slaven Rezic for the report); echo, printf, test, [, true
        and false inside Perl as coreutils 9.4 (X21, partly; IP1, IP2);
        Windows: win_priority and win_job (W4), SetChildShowWindow, more than
        64 children, X34 fixed; on_write gets the errno; X27: the run's
        environment varied, stand-ins and fixed file times (EV1); the run-time
        comparison redone and its table replaced (A1(b)); the comparison and
        the snippet library through Run; X33, X35 fixed.  The figures of
        post4-0.04 hold (the measured files differ only in $VERSION); digest
        of the code they depend on:
        4185f0fc85d210c5d14e02c79c998ea586198496e58b4e1caf7a9154c969f11e.
      - X36 (ours; RT #182809, Slaven Rezic: 'Undefined symbol "av_count"',
        2026-11-08; thanks to Slaven): since 0.01 Spawner.xs used av_count(),
        which perl has only since 5.33.1, so with perl 5.30 (and any perl
        before 5.34, though MIN_PERL_VERSION is 5.020) Spawner.so did not load
        and every test failed.  Spawner.xs defines it where perl does not, as
        perl does (AvFILL + 1).  Checked with perl 5.30.3 built here (Linux):
        0.09 fails to load as reported; now the suite passes; the XS also
        compiles against perl 5.20.3's headers with no undeclared function
        (that perl itself did not build here, so the suite was not run on
        it).  FreeBSD itself not tested here.
      - X21, partly (the user, 2026-11-08: "echo, printf, test", "inside the
        Perl process"): Proc::ShellSpawn::Programs does echo, printf, test,
        [, true and false as GNU coreutils 9.4 does them, in Perl (Perl's
        sprintf and file tests, Math::BigInt for the 64-bit range checks,
        test's grammar as a recursive descent), with output and messages in
        the program's order (glibc's error() flushes stdout first) and its
        write errors (SIGPIPE, "write error").  Decision IP1 (the user: "when
        a well-debugged builtin is already present, use it.  (By default;
        Configurable.)"): Proc::ShellSpawn runs them inside Perl on every
        platform; PROC_SHELLSPAWN_INPROC=none runs the programs (or names
        only some), and %Proc::ShellSpawn::INPROC can be changed.  command -v
        names them where there is no program.  Checked against coreutils 9.4
        on 12,348 runs (handwritten and generated cases, C and UTF-8
        locales) and on 3,000 random words for %q; t/programs.t replays 2,284
        recorded runs (also under Wine with Strawberry Perl: pass) and
        compares the shell layer with the programs and in-process; the suite
        passes both ways (t/dash.t: 550 commands of the batteries).
        Differences: --help and --version texts, printf's floating
        conversions in double (coreutils: long double), and %q, where
        coreutils 9.4 has a ❰BUG❱ (gnulib's quotearg keeps a stale state when
        it starts a word over: an extra '', or a missing $' that makes the
        shell read the word back wrong); ours follows gnulib's fixed rule
        (decision IP2, the user: "Use the builtin").
      - Proc::Spawner::on_write: the callback gets the errno of a failed
        write (it got nothing); Proc::Spawner::Graph's perl_write passes it
        in $!.
      - W4 (the user, 2026-11-08: "(c) and (b); (c) beats (b) if present"):
        on Windows, win_priority (a priority class) and win_job (job limits
        by Windows' names: ProcessMemoryLimit, JobMemoryLimit,
        PerProcessUserTimeLimit, PerJobUserTimeLimit, ActiveProcessLimit),
        exact, per child; nice and rlimit mapped to them, approximately (nice
        to the class with the nearest nice value; AS, DATA, CPU, NPROC to job
        limits; other resources unsupported); win_* win where both are
        given, and are unsupported elsewhere.  The class is set on the
        suspended child (Wine ignores it as a creation flag).  Checked under
        Wine with a child printing its class; Wine stores no job limits, so
        theirs is not checked; the mapping is tested in t/spawner.t.
      - Windows (the TODO's work items; checked under Wine with Strawberry
        Perl 5.38.2): Win32::SetChildShowWindow is honoured as by Perl's own
        spawn (a child printing its startup info: the same through both
        primitives); more than 64 children at once work (100 checked: the
        loop polls each child; Perl's own spawn still stops at 64, its
        table); Makefile.PL skips the POSIX probes there, and perl
        Makefile.PL, gmake (no warning) and gmake test of t/windows.t and
        t/parsers.t pass with Strawberry Perl's own gcc and gmake.  nice and
        rlimit there are decisions (TODO A.12).
      - X34 ❰BUG❱ (ours; 0.07 to 0.09, Windows code): the kept process
        handles were in a fixed table of 1,024, and a child started while it
        was full lost its status if it ended before it was waited for (76 of
        1,100 under Wine); the table grows now (the same check: none lost).
      - TODO: two pending decisions added (a missing Run in M2; keeping
        Perl's reopen of the graph's own pipes).
      - X27, the run's environment varied (2026-11-08): the six kinds of
        workaround (nothing, a second run with the other case, fixtures,
        chosen values, stand-ins for programs, leaving samples out) listed
        per code in doc/large-subset/large-subset-environment.md, section 9.
        large-subset-runcompare.pl -env: posix (POSIXLY_CORRECT=1), utf8,
        sigpipe (ignored), noproc (/proc hidden), tmp (a fresh tmpfs on /tmp,
        /var/tmp, /dev/shm per run), random (a fixed file over /dev/urandom),
        private (empty /home, /root, /mnt, /media, /srv, /var), dev (a
        private /dev); the mounts in a mount namespace of each run's own.
        -base (the mounting variants need one outside what they hide),
        -only @FILE; ours runs from a copy of lib/, blib/, t/try.pl.
        large-subset-runreport.pl -b BASE (each sample's class against a base
        run's, and what changed) and -c.  Each variant on its code's samples
        against two base runs (the noise): POSIXLY_CORRECT turns the
        program echo from bash's side to dash's (29) and makes ours differ
        from both for \x (2); C.UTF-8 changes one printf "\u20AC"; SIGPIPE
        ignored changes only messages and 6 timeouts; /proc hidden makes the
        3 samples using /dev/stdin, /dev/stderr fail alike; a fresh /tmp
        makes 6 agree whose shells met each other's files there (or another
        sample's: /tmp is shared across the whole run); a fixed
        /dev/urandom makes all 25 agree; private and dev change nothing.
        Every changed sample: doc/large-subset/tools/
        large-subset-env-variants.tsv.  The levels are not changed by it
        (TODO, A1 and A2a).  bash 5.2.21 does not emulate /dev/stdin and the like
        without /proc (the document said it did; corrected).
      - X33 (ours, in large-subset-runcompare.pl since post4-0.04): each
        run's output and error files belonged to root, so /dev/stdout and
        /dev/stderr, which Linux reopens by path, were not writable for the
        run's user ("Permission denied" in all three shells; one sample
        affected in practice, tee /dev/stderr).  Fixed: the files are given
        to the run's user first.
      - X27, EV1 (the user, 2026-11-08): the runs with the environment
        varied are footnotes to the level totals
        (large-subset-environment.md, section 1; README), not levels.
      - X27, stand-ins and fixed file times (the user, 2026-11-08: their
        answers controlled by the environment): large-subset-runcompare.pl
        -env fail (every program the run only logs exits with
        STANDIN_STATUS, default 1), output (prints STANDIN_OUTPUT, default
        "one  two\n*\n\nlast"), mtime (the run's copy of the fixture gets the
        times FIXTURE_MTIME, default 2020-01-01); the values from the
        runner's own environment, recorded in OUTDIR/env.txt.  Run on the
        samples of prog-status (113), prog-output (1,014) and time (223)
        against two base runs: status 1 changes nothing beyond the messages;
        the chosen output parts dash and bash in 47 samples, none with ours
        against both (cd and kill given several words, test ==, all decided
        or known; and X35); fixed times cut the changes between two runs of
        those samples from 50 to 32 (the rest: the shells' own clocks,
        times outside the run's copy).  large-subset-environment.md,
        section 9 and footnote 2; large-subset-env-variants.tsv.
      - X35 (ours; fixed as dash, the user, 2026-11-08): unset of a word that
        is not a name (from an expansion; the parser rejects a literal one)
        did nothing with status 0.  Now, as dash, it stops the shell with
        "unset: WORD: bad variable name" and status 2, the names before it
        already unset; unset -f takes any word.  bash takes such a word for a
        function's name.  6 strings in t/batteries/largebattery.txt.
      - large-subset-runcompare.pl -run RUNLIB: ours goes through Run (as
        where there is neither posix_spawn nor fork: M2, the primitive
        system1), PROC_SPAWNER_MISSING="posix_spawn fork" for ours alone;
        t/try.pl takes the names in TRY_HIDE out of the environment once the
        modules are loaded, so that the samples do not see it.
      - The whole snippet library through Run (the user, 2026-11-08): run
        twice in the same environment (-env tmp,random,mtime), ours through
        posix_spawn and ours through Run 0.07 (-run): the tables agree, and
        sample by sample ours changed in 53, all by timing, process IDs or
        clocks (49 of them with the shells changing too); no difference comes
        from Run, so no Run bug to fix.  Run's tests and Proc-Spawner's suite
        through Run (also RUN_AS_WIN32=1) pass.  large-subset-state.md,
        section 7, "Through Run".
      - A1(b) (the user, 2026-11-08): the run-time comparison redone with a
        fresh /tmp, a fixed /dev/urandom and fixed file times (-env
        tmp,random,mtime) replaces post4-0.04's table: same 15,861 (15,751),
        messages only 23,311 (23,396), ours differs 3 (7), the shells differ
        1,220 (1,239), timeouts 15 (17).  The 3: printf -v (2, Q2) and echo
        into a broken pipe in a subshell (1); of post4-0.04's other 4,
        printf '%0.s-' now agrees (Z0), /dev/urandom agrees, the other 2 were
        chance.  large-subset-state.md, section 7 (also the external calls),
        README, large-subset-run-differences.tsv, test-pools.md; the X27
        cross-check (large-subset-environment.md, section 5) comes out the
        same.  The atomic-call tables stay those of post4-0.04.

0.09  2026-11-08
      - Since 0.08: what posix_spawn has no option for (rlimit, sigignore,
        nice: M1); no posix_spawn at all (M2): fork and exec, and where there
        is no fork either, Run 0.07 (repository ilyaz-ai/run; to be released
        first, recommended in META) applies the redirections around Perl's
        own spawn; Windows: the kept process handle released, sigignore of
        PIPE and INT, t/windows.t; ulimit through getrlimit; X29 fixed, X31
        fixed, X32 recorded; known issue X27 classified; TODO; the documents
        cross-linked.  The figures of post4-0.04 hold (the measured files
        differ only in $VERSION); digest of the code they depend on:
        80e40b1ce413ea4c39203a0c5bbd134b800477da8641dca26dc55aa01e693d41.
      - Known issue X32 (ours; possible, not observed): without /proc, Perl's
        own writes into a pipe the caller gave are blocking writes of
        PIPE_BUF bytes after select, and block if a child fills the pipe in
        between.  In the known issues, Proc::Spawner's LIMITATIONS, README
        and TODO.
      - The Markdown documents link to each other and to the files they name
        (398 links, relative; none broken): a path or file name in a code
        span became a link, a script followed by its arguments links its
        name.  Mentions of the old Project's files link to the branch
        project-2026-11-03 where they are there (19, checked through the
        GitHub API; the repository is private, so they work for those who
        can see it).  doc/perl-win32-system-bugs.md now ships (a shipped
        document links to it), so every relative link of a shipped document
        points into the distribution.
      - M1 (the user, 2026-11-06: what posix_spawn has no option for, done
        around the call where it can be undone, failed for the rest): new
        settings sigignore (signals the child ignores), rlimit (its resource
        limits) and nice (an increment), in start and spawn.  The XSUB sets
        them in this process right around its call, in C, with every signal
        blocked, and puts them back right after.  Unsupported (dropped or
        failed with ENOTSUP, as UNSUPPORTED says): a hard limit lower than
        ours without CAP_SYS_RESOURCE, a higher nice value without the right
        to lower it back, a CPU limit this process has used up, ignoring
        SIGCHLD; all three on Windows.  Checked as root (here without
        CAP_SYS_RESOURCE) and as an unprivileged user; t/spawner.t.  A memory
        limit below what this process uses makes glibc's posix_spawn fail
        with ENOMEM (documented).
      - M2 (the user, 2026-11-06: no posix_spawn at all, as Run 0.06's
        forkless mode): Makefile.PL probes posix_spawn itself; without it,
        or with PROC_SPAWNER_MISSING=posix_spawn, the primitive is the new
        XSUB __spawn_nowait, fork and exec in C with nothing in between, and
        every other setting is done around the call.  The whole suite passes
        so, and a build without posix_spawn passes t/spawner.t and t/dash.t.
        t/missing-matrix.pl: profile "no posix_spawn"; the Windows profile
        has the M1 settings.
      - Stale since 0.07: "the shell layer needs posix_spawn's chdir"
        (README, X10, t/dash.t).  chdir is done around the call where the
        libc lacks it; checked by building without chdir and fchdir: t/dash.t
        passes, 13 of 13.
      - M2, no fork either (the user, 2026-11-07): the redirections are
        delegated to Run 0.07 (repository ilyaz-ai/run; to be released
        first), whose start() applies them around Perl's own spawn, system {
        FILE } 1, ARGV (P_NOWAIT), as on OS/2, Win32 and VMS, and puts our
        fds back ($Proc::Spawner::PRIMITIVE 'system1'; chdir and fchdir are
        done here).  PROC_SPAWNER_MISSING="posix_spawn fork" selects it on
        Linux too, where Run emulates the spawn by fork and exec: the whole
        suite passes so.  The arguments go to Perl as they are: Perl's command
        line on Windows does not quote them as the C runtime parses them back
        (❰BUG❱ in Perl; doc/perl-win32-system-bugs.md, to be reported), so the
        tests of exact argv and of batch files are TODO there (the user: no
        bug-for-bug workaround).  The program is looked up here (posix_spawn's
        meaning of FILE and search).  The child sees fds 0-2 only (dup2 or
        open onto fd 3 and up unsupported); pidfd, a non-empty sigmask,
        fchdir, a group to join unsupported; at most 64 children (EAGAIN).
        New t/windows.t (Windows only): both primitives under Wine with
        Strawberry Perl 5.38.2, 13 passing, and the 2 TODO with system1.
        OS/2 and VMS not tested.  A Run that is missing or too old is now an
        error of spawn (before: any exception inside became EINVAL).
      - Windows, both primitives: sigignore of PIPE (no SIGPIPE there) and of
        INT in a new process group (Ctrl-C is off there) need nothing; other
        signals stay unsupported (Perl's kill ends a Windows child, or sends
        Ctrl-Break, which it cannot ignore).
      - ulimit (the shell layer) reads the limits with getrlimit, through
        Proc::Spawner's XSUB, instead of /proc/self/limits or, without /proc,
        asking a shell (the user, 2026-11-07: the module has XS anyway).  Same
        output as dash for -n -Hn -f -s -c -t -v -l -m -d; t/dash.t passes
        with /proc hidden too.
      - X29 ❰BUG❱ (ours; 0.07, 0.08): where the fd actions are done around the
        call, the copies of our fds went above an open action's flags plus
        1000, beyond the common limit of 1024 fds, and the call failed with
        EINVAL; now just above the highest fd the actions name (the 1000 was
        never needed).  Found on Windows; checked on Linux with ulimit -n 1024.
      - Windows: __dup_high (a copy of an fd above a number) works there.
      - Windows: the process handle kept for a child's exit code is released
        once the loop has the status (Proc::Spawner::reaped, also for callers
        of the raw spawn who wait themselves).  Checked under Wine with
        Strawberry Perl 5.38.2: the handle is gone after the status (0.08:
        still open); 1,100 children in a row give their status.
      - X31 (ours; only in the Windows code, which no release builds yet):
        Proc::Spawner's open action on Windows (win_spawn.h) did not map
        /dev/null to NUL, as Perl's own open does.  Fixed; checked under Wine
        from a directory on C: (on Wine's Z:, the Linux root, the bug hides):
        a test program of win_spawn.h failed before and passes after, and
        t/windows.t (new test) passes with both primitives.  Perl's other
        Windows special cases checked against ours: nothing else missing.
      - X11 and X12 closed (the user, 2026-11-07): the Windows primitive
        exists (win_spawn.h; M2's system1), and the corpora were run through
        the executor by the run-time comparison of post4-0.04.
      - TODO: every open thing in one place, by who has to act (the
        decisions waiting for the author first); linked from README.
      - Known issue X27 classified (the user's method, 2026-11-07: parse
        all the samples in one batch, split them into atomic blocks marked
        with their samples, and pass only what is shown unaffected, so that
        a pass is definite).  New doc/large-subset/tools/
        large-subset-blocks.pl sorts the 40,410 accepted samples from the
        parse trees alone, programs taken as real and opaque, into strict
        (365: nothing in the environment can change the comparison, given
        only how the shell is started), held (2: the same, under assumptions
        known to hold in every tested environment, such as /dev/null),
        almost strict (34,291: the same under named assumptions, mostly that
        a program does the same in each run and the shells look it up
        alike) and undecided (5,752,
        each with the codes of what it may depend on: files, variables, a
        program's output or status taken by the shell, /tmp, ...).  $? takes
        the status of what ran before it, set -e makes every later status
        decide (the user, 2026-11-07); absolute paths are told apart by the
        area they are in (path-tmp, path-system, ...), and whether the run
        could control it; /dev/null is an assumption known to hold in every
        environment tested (the level "held": dash and bash have it, ours on
        Windows gets NUL, X31).  The record,
        one line per sample by snippet ID with a short code, is
        doc/large-subset/large-subset-provenance.tsv; the codes, how to
        remake it and the findings: doc/large-subset/
        large-subset-environment.md.  Checked by 84 probes, by reading all
        strict samples and random others, and against the run's
        differences.  It replaces the first version of the same day, which
        tagged what it recognised and passed the rest (81.4 %): its passes
        were not definite.
      - The batch check (-batch): the samples joined into one text parse as
        they do alone, but for the 198 that change how the text after them
        parses (functions, aliases, P1, a trailing backslash, exec) and 2
        whose arithmetic text keeps its trailing blank (large grammar) or not
        (base grammar), same value.  New known issue X30 (ours): with $LINENO
        in a text, the parse time grows with the square of its length (the
        joined text: 89 s, 8.4 s without the one $LINENO).

0.08  2026-11-06
      - Since 0.07: Windows (EXPERIMENTAL): win_spawn.h compared with Perl's
        own spawn and fixed; the module built with MinGW-w64 against
        Strawberry Perl 5.38.2 and run under Wine; batch files; the options of
        %Proc::Spawner::WINDOWS.  Fixed on every system: spawn's result in
        scalar context when a setting is missing.  The figures of post4-0.04
        hold (the measured files differ only in $VERSION); digest of the code
        they depend on: 3956c46c16d3290baf27f9475da8b72d431f17a194b2ba9fba91e3e0fe863bb8.
      - Windows, run under Wine with Strawberry Perl 5.38.2 (Spawner.dll built
        with MinGW-w64): spawn, waitpid, options work.  New: batch files run
        through the system's cmd.exe, arguments quoted as Rust's standard
        library does since BatBadBut (CR, LF refused); %Proc::Spawner::WINDOWS:
        new_group (Ctrl-C reaches the parent, not the children; default on),
        kill_with_parent (a job object that ends the children with the parent;
        default on), new_console (as Perl's spawn; default on); console
        pseudo-handles of Windows 7 kept out of the handle list.  Fixed: spawn
        returned its count of results in scalar context when a setting was
        missing (now the pid); Proc::Spawner no longer uses fcntl and SIGCHLD
        on Windows (the loop polls there).  doc/pspawn-spawner.md, section 8:
        how the child sees its arguments, Perl's system against ours.
      - win_spawn.h compared with Perl's own spawn on Windows (win32/win32.c of
        perl 5.38.2) and fixed: PATHEXT's extensions before the bare name, and
        quoted PATH entries, as Perl's qualified_path(); cmd.exe /c quoted as
        Perl's create_command_line() does; the environment block sorted, with
        the drives' directories (=C:) kept; E2BIG over 32766 characters;
        strings not held as UTF-8 read in the ANSI code page (as Perl's
        CreateProcessA); the default environment and directory are Perl's
        (PerlEnv_get_childenv, PerlEnv_get_childdir); its own allocator on the
        process heap (found by compiling against Strawberry Perl 5.38.2, where
        malloc and free are Perl's macros).  Spawner.dll compiles and links
        with MinGW-w64 against Strawberry Perl's headers and libperl538.a; not
        yet loaded.  Open questions and the deliberate differences:
        doc/pspawn-spawner.md, section 8.

0.07  2026-11-05
      - Since 0.06: what the spawn primitive lacks is done around the call
