0.06  2026-11-05
      - Since 0.05, the same day: the fixes for systems without /proc, and the
        measurement of the atomic calls extended.  The figures of post4-0.04
        hold (the measured files differ only in $VERSION); digest of the code
        they depend on:
        c19ac884428cba12a90ff7e8d1bfc4dba7b540e621dc72062563a5ce4ec950b1.
      - RT #182479 (Slaven Rezic: the tests fail on FreeBSD, which has no
        /proc and no /bin/true; thanks to Slaven for the report and for his
        smokers).  The tests find programs through PATH, read
        signal dispositions, masks and process groups from a Perl child
        instead of /proc, and skip what needs Linux's /proc (t/graph.t's
        reporter, the batteries fdbattery and sigenv, pidfd without
        /proc/self/fdinfo).  Running the suite with /proc hidden found three
        faults of the library itself without /proc, now fixed: (1) Perl's
        writes into a pipe it shares with children set O_NONBLOCK on the
        shared open file, so the children's writes failed with EAGAIN and
        output was lost (known issue L10's fallback); a pipe of ours is now a
        named FIFO opened once more for Perl alone, and a pipe we were given
        takes PIPE_BUF bytes per blocking write; (2) the check whether an fd
        is close-on-exec read /proc/self/fdinfo, and took every fd as
        inherited without it (echo x >&5 then failed like the program, not
        like dash): now fcntl, through the new XSUB
        Proc::Spawner::__fd_cloexec; (3) ulimit died ("Undefined subroutine
        Proc::ShellSpawn::Large::unsupported") without /proc/self/limits; it
        now asks a shell, which has Perl's limits: dash, bash --posix, ksh or
        sh, in /bin first, then on PATH (none: "not supported", status 248).
        Known issue X28.
      - The atomic calls where no program runs (large-subset-builtins.pl) now
        include commands not found and settings alone ($ONBUILTIN reports
        them too), with messages as dash's, bash's or neither; the external
        calls of every sample in the classes of the main table
        (large-subset-runreport.pl -x).

0.05  2026-11-05
      - The release of post4-0.04, with the version number 0.05 and
        NOTES-post3-0.04.txt no longer shipped.  The figures of post4-0.04
        hold (the measured files differ only in $VERSION); digest of the code
        they depend on:
        743121ae0b5fb7af55b5308fb51c3db9cf61ca74ec2d3a40e98a3dc6cca634cd.
      - In short, since 0.04: functions before regular builtins (3A); rule-E
        choices; background jobs go on between run() calls (K3); aliases
        known only when they run (P1); rule F ([ a \< b ]); read with names
        from expansions; bracket expressions with quotes, expansions or '/';
        K0 (kill with no process ID); bugs X25, X26 and others fixed; the
        behaviour of every accepted corpus sample compared with dash and bash
        at run time (doc/large-subset/large-subset-state.md, section 7).
      The documents label the unreleased states between 0.04 and 0.05 post-0.04
      to post4-0.04; their entries follow, newest first.

    post4-0.04:
      - Bracket expressions with quoted members, expansions or '/' (the work
        list, large-subset-state.md section 4; both shells agree, probes
        brquote, brslash): grep ^[\>\<], ls [a"]"], ^["$v"], [$v], and
        something[^/]*.  Quoted characters are members, never operators
        (also ! ^ - ]); an unquoted expansion's characters are active.  In
        pathname expansion a bracket expression with a '/' is none: its '['
        is literal, as both shells.  Also in the pattern of ${x%[...]}.  The
        large grammar only; Proc::ShellSubset itself still rejects them.
      - In ${x:-word} (not a pattern), * ? [ are accepted: literal where the
        ${...} is quoted ("${1:-sure? [y/N]}"), active where it is not
        (probe dqpword).
      - Case patterns and ${x%p} are put together whole before they become a
        regex, so a bracket expression may span quoted and unquoted parts.
      - Fixed: [^...] (rule E: negation) was turned into [!...] by the
        parser, so a word that matched no file came out as [!...] instead of
        as written.  The parser now keeps '^'; the executor reads it.
      - Fixed: unset NAME[...] was accepted and did nothing, with status 0;
        dash stops with "bad variable name", bash unsets an array element
        (probe unsetarr).  It is rejected now, as bash's arrays are; known
        issue X26.  New detector feature unsetarr (B); unset foo][ is B by
        hand (probe unsetbad: dash fails, bash returns 0).
      - Proc::ShellSpawn: $ONBUILTIN, a code ref called before each builtin
        or function runs (for measurement; t/try.pl logs to the file named by
        RECBUILTIN); doc/large-subset/tools/large-subset-builtins.pl runs every
        builtin call of the corpus alone in dash, bash and ours;
        large-subset-runreport.pl -x compares the external calls one by one.
      - The behaviour figures in separate tables, with and without a
        coreutils program involved (large-subset-runreport.pl -q), and the
        atomic calls: every call the corpus makes to echo, printf, test, [,
        kill or pwd, run as dash's builtin, bash's builtin and the program
        (large-subset-threeway.pl; the database large-subset-threeway-db.jsonl,
        757 calls; calls made by xargs are left out).  With K0, dash and bash
        agree and ours differs in 7 samples of 40,410 (first run: 57).  The
        messages are compared as multisets of characters (the shells' messages
        of pipeline elements mix).  README: a summary, near the top.
      - The Git history now has the CPAN releases 0.01 to 0.04 (tags v0.01 to
        v0.04); CPAN's 0.04 is the tree once called "the pre-release snapshot".
        doc/pspawn-spawner.md, section 8, and the post-0.04 entry below: the
        Windows section was never in a CPAN release.
      - K0 (the user, 2026-11-05): kill with a signal (-N, -NAME, -s NAME) or
        --, and no process ID (kill $(pgrep x) when nothing matches), is
        answered by us as by both shells: dash's usage text, status 2 (the
        program kill: a long usage, status 1).  large-subset-state.md, section
        1: the rules are a triage of the past; two principles of least
        surprise in conflict (a tool called in a script as when called alone;
        a tool's own treatment of rare edge cases), and the per-tool
        decisions (K0, rule F, Q2).  Known issue X27 (TODO): which samples of
        the run-time comparison depend on the environment.
      - New: the behaviour of the accepted samples, run in dash, bash --posix
        and ours (tools/large-subset-runcompare.pl, large-subset-runreport.pl;
        large-subset-state.md section 7): of 40,410, all three agree in
        15,760, only the messages differ in 23,368, and dash and bash agree
        while ours differs in 33 (29: kill with no process ID, the program's
        status 1 against the shells' 2; question for the user).
      - Fixed, found so: a string with no command, or only comments, was
        rejected by run() (the parser option 'empty' was missing from
        run()'s options), though the measurement counted it as accepted;
        eval of empty text stopped with status 248; kill -s WINCH $$ (also
        CHLD, URG, CONT, kill -0 $$) ended the shell with status 156.
      - Figures: 44 samples rejected within the lower yardstick (post3-0.04:
        49; 3 accepted now, 2 reclassified as blatant by unsetarr); digest
        aa2734f2147be58073d44713de7f003cdee525f593d5e90f70f9f64e900f08bc.
        Tests LS:319-323; battery lines in t/batteries/large-subset-post04.txt.

    post3-0.04:
      - Aliases known only when they run (P1, the user, 2026-11-03: "go with
        better aliases"): where an alias definition has an expansion, or stands
        in a compound command, a function, an and-or list, a pipeline or the
        background, the parser stops after that line; the executor parses the
        rest of the text when it gets there, with the aliases defined by then,
        as both shells read a text one complete command at a time.  Tree node
        { rest => TEXT, lineoff => N }.
      - Rule F (the user, 2026-11-03: where a utility is implemented wrongly,
        we may do better): [ s1 \< s2 ] and [ s1 \> s2 ] (also test, also after
        !), which both shells run and the coreutils program refuses, are
        compared by the executor, by bytes; the option test_lt_gt is needed
        only for longer expressions with < or >.
      - doc/large-subset/large-subset-state.md, section 4: what POSIX says of
        the ${...} forms both shells refuse (an expansion error, or
        unspecified).
      - Figures: 49 samples rejected within the lower yardstick (post2-0.04:
        72); digest
        b189e43efd804af3e15e4224d2e7f46a890d7723301dd792fd9094ec8aaf6c59.

    post2-0.04:
      - read takes names from expansions (read $v, read "$v"), checked when it
        runs, with dash's messages and status 2; a read-only name no longer ends
        the shell (as dash).  ${##}, ${#?}, ${#!}, ${#$} accepted (both shells).
      - Fixed: a name from an expansion in export "$v" was never checked against
        the names the subset may not set (the test was always true).
      - Measurement: 14 samples left out by hand as noise
        (doc/large-subset/tools/large-subset-noise.txt, read by
        large-subset-drops.pl; the user, 2026-11-03); the detector counts the
        decided rows N1-N3 (${x:?}, a special builtin's error, command -v) as
        class X, as large-subset-logical.md says of every decided row.
      - Figures: 72 samples rejected within the lower yardstick (post-0.04: 91);
        digest (now also over large-subset-noise.txt)
        a23490770df33a87e8cbc1008d7d7301c7bcf567cb56bd2d5b5e13a94e0c4636.

    post-0.04:
      - 3A (the user, 2026-11-03): a function comes before a regular builtin of
        its name and before the commands run as programs (POSIX order).  Functions
        may be named cd, read, jobs, echo, kill, pwd, ...; such calls are checked
        at run time (Proc::ShellSpawn::Large::b_late); command NAME reaches the
        regular builtins we run.  Not alias, unalias.
      - Rule E choices done: ${@#p}, ${*%p} on each parameter (G4, as bash);
        $LINENO (G8, as bash; not in eval or trap text); UID an ordinary
        variable where the string sets it (G15, as dash); ~ without HOME from
        the password file (G16, as bash), and HOME may be set and unset; break
        and continue in a subshell inside a loop end it (as dash).  jobs takes
        %N, %%, %+, %-.
      - K3 (the user's choice B, 2026-11-03): background jobs go on between
        run() calls too: while they remain, Proc::ShellSpawn keeps a deferred
        SIGCHLD handler installed, which calls the new Proc::Spawner::poll().
        Cost (documented in doc/pspawn-design.md, section 6, and the POD): the
        program's own sleep and select return early when a child ends.  Test
        t/between.t.
      - Q2 decided (the user's choice B): printf -v stays with the program.
      - doc/checklists/corpus-gaps.html and its sources (doc/large-subset/pages/)
        are no longer shipped: all its questions are decided (they are in 0.04).
      - X25 fixed (a bug since 0.01): break or continue in a pipeline element
        inside a loop died with a Perl error.
      - Hand-kept verdict "no difference" for the new parser test LS:299
        (kill() { :; }; kill -SIGHUP 1): the detector flags kill -SIGHUP, but
        both shells run the function (probe fnkill).
      - doc/pspawn-spawner.md: section 8 (Windows) is in the distribution.
        (Corrected post4-0.04: it was not "in 0.01 but lost by 0.04"; no CPAN
        release had it, only the Project's later copy of 0.01.)
      - Detector: atpat, lineno, nohome, uidassign, SC3058 are class X (rule E);
        uidread is the old bashvar for UID.  Battery
        t/batteries/large-subset-post04.txt.
      - Figures: 91 samples rejected within the lower yardstick (0.04: 95);
        digest 1b9eac74d672e79d89764f71b1a6125e4a52f4cdc42943a6db39ec3d0b6d95aa.

0.04  2026-11-03
      - The documents of only historical interest are no longer shipped; they
        are in Proc-Spawner 0.03 on CPAN: doc/large-subset/large-subset-
        {ceiling,investigation,marks,upstream-reports}.md, doc/pspawn-run-
        shortcomings.md, doc/pspawn-upstream-reports.md, doc/parsers/shell-
        subset-corpus-stats.md.  References to them say so.
      - The decision pages that are no longer relevant are no longer shipped
        either (they are in 0.03): in doc/checklists/ child-exit, run-graph-api,
        spawn-graph, large-subset and dialect-choices.html, and in
        doc/large-subset/pages/ their sources and build.pl.  Only
        corpus-gaps.html (question Q2 is open) and its sources stay.
        Known issue X1 now names the alternatives of K3 itself.
      - The figures of post4-0.03 were checked again on 0.04: the same per-sample
        results; they are labelled 0.04.  Digest of the code they depend on:
        ecde8b9b9610680560a69e56313f01687f29e51688bcd029c6cef837fa03eeac.
      The documents label the unreleased states between 0.03 and 0.04 post-0.03
      to post4-0.03; their entries follow, newest first.

    post4-0.03:
      - Rule E (the user, 2026-11-03), replacing A3: where dash and bash
        differ, the subset takes the plainly logical behaviour, and rejects the
        form only where there is none.  doc/large-subset/large-subset-logical.md
        lists every difference with its category (info, error, posix, same,
        harm, platform, meaning, effect, none) and checks the earlier decisions
        against the rule.  Done so far: jobs, jobs -p, jobs -l list the jobs also
        in a pipeline and in $( ) (as bash; this replaces the rejection of
        post3-0.03); return outside a function is an error (as bash); cd with
        extra operands or without HOME is an error (as bash); descriptors above 9
        (option fdmulti now on by default); [^...] negates; patterns never match
        . or ..; settings with a redirection are redirected before their values
        are expanded (as dash).  getopts leaves OPTARG unset when it finds no option, or
        an option without an argument (POSIX; as bash; dash keeps or empties it).  Battery t/batteries/large-subset-e.txt (the
        differences from dash are listed in t/dash-known.txt as rule E).
      - Forms both shells run alike, now accepted: reading $PPID (assigning it
        stays rejected), export "$name", symbolic umask modes, redirections on
        cd, and the top-level jobs -p.
      - X19 fixed: a trap's action now runs before the next command of the list
        (it could run after it, about 1 run in 30).  New known issue X24.
      - The detector: the choices of rule E are class X with basis E:CATEGORY;
        ShellCheck's SC3023 and SC3026 likewise; local and return outside a
        function are separate features; local=... is no longer taken for local.
      - Figures: 95 samples rejected within the lower yardstick (post3-0.03:
        106); digest 84245e1d9a2ad88da10b320f8d2e94ddeb0b12bd888925555b920d985026e10a.

    post3-0.03:
      - Proc::ShellSubset::Large rejects jobs in a pipeline, a command
        substitution or a compound command: there dash lists nothing and bash
        the jobs (probes jobspipe, jobsppipe, jobspsubst).  Tests LS:288-292.
      - The detector has two more blatant features: jobs in a pipeline or $( )
        (jobsctx), and cd with extra operands (cdargs: dash ignores them, bash
        fails).  Its word pattern now keeps $( ), backquotes, ${ } and
        backslash-escapes in one word.  11 new probes (jobs, cd, the P6 forms) in
        doc/large-subset/dialect-cases/large-subset-dialect-probes2.txt.
      - Figures: 106 samples rejected within the lower yardstick (post-0.03:
        117); digest 64810b85d9f4cc777ef7d41f3cc3f9f4262c1c56cf877ba00489dbce07b117e8.

    post2-0.03:
      - pwd runs as the program /bin/pwd -L, with PWD set to the shell's own
        directory, and its operands dropped (known issue X23; the user's choice C,
        2026-11-03): after cd through a symbolic link it prints the directory as
        reached, as both shells (before: the physical path).  Two more lines in
        t/batteries/large-subset-p6.txt (21 of 21 agree with dash).  The parsers
        did not change, so the figures of post-0.03 hold.

    post-0.03:
      - Proc::ShellSubset::Large accepts more forms both shells run alike (item P6
        of "Shell Corpus Gaps"; probes in doc/large-subset/dialect-cases):
        read -p PROMPT (also -rp; the executor writes the prompt to fd 2 only
        when fd 0 is a terminal, as both shells); cd -P and cd -L (the last one
        counts; the tree gets cd_p => 1, and the executor resolves symbolic
        links); a pattern in a redirection target is literal (echo a >*.log
        writes the file '*.log', as dash and bash in POSIX mode); set -o
        ignoreeof, notify, nolog, set -b and set +m (nothing to do); trap with
        signal names in any case and signal numbers, also FPE, and KILL and STOP
        (cannot be caught: nothing to do, status 0).  Tests LS:273-287 (LS:114
        now accepted), and t/batteries/large-subset-p6.txt (19 of 19 agree with
        dash).
      - pwd with an option both shells refuse (pwd -x, pwd --help) is rejected
        (rule Q1; test LS:111 changed).  Known issue X23: a bare pwd after cd
        through a symbolic link differs from both shells (the program /bin/pwd,
        decision B2 of the base parser's checklist) - a question to the user.
      - The detector (tools/large-subset-features.pl): a read -p prompt with
        quotes no longer hides the options after it (read -p "`echo -e x`" m
        was taken for read -e), read -p PROMPT without a name is the bash-only
        REPLY form, and set -o pipefail and the like are seen after other
        options (set -euo pipefail).
      - Figures (doc/large-subset/large-subset-state.md): the samples rejected
        within the lower yardstick went from 163 (0.03) to 117.  They depend on
        the parsers, the measurement scripts and the verdicts, whose sha256
        (the command: doc/large-subset/large-subset-state.md, section 3) is
        4921dfbd1b838bc48dabe234a5111ad69d8fbb7d43c8278c72dcf74a5ad9b7bf.

0.03  2026-11-03
      - Proc::ShellSubset::Large accepts forms dash and bash run alike (items
        P1-P4 of "Shell Corpus Gaps"): '{' and '}' inside words where no brace
        expansion can start; $( ), `...`, ${#NAME} and $((...)) inside $((...));
        setting PS1, PS4, HOSTNAME, PIPESTATUS and BASH; export $v[=value];
        exit N above 255; umask -S; command -p PROGRAM.  '{' or '}' as a
        command name, and '{NAME}' right before a redirection (bash: a named
        descriptor), stay rejected (tests LS:212-245).
      - Proc::ShellSpawn, Proc::ShellSpawn::Large: run those forms ($( ) inside
        $(( )) expanded first; exit N modulo 256; umask -S; command -p with
        /usr/bin:/bin; export with a name from an expansion, checked at run time).
      - The borrowed test pool: the scripts that made
        t/parser/shell-subset-corpus-tests.pl are now in doc/parsers/tools/
        (extract, run, group, gentests, prov, verify and the reports), with
        the grouping of 2026-10-29 (groups.tsv); doc/test-pools.md says how to
        rebuild every pool and measurement from the pinned sources (checked:
        all 612 snippet IDs restore their texts exactly).
      - Measuring against dash and bash by the shells alone:
        doc/large-subset/large-subset-state.md (the rules, which beats which,
        the figures, the work list); large-subset-dialect-features.md (every
        probed feature, blatant or subtle, with who decided it);
        tools/large-subset-features.pl (the detector, and the per-test record
        t/parser/large-subset-shells.tsv), tools/large-subset-verdicts.txt
        (verdicts kept by hand, postponed ones as free text), and a new
        tools/large-subset-yardsticks.pl (the old one sorted our parser's
        own messages).
      - doc/pspawn-known-issues.md: X21 (the programs echo, [, printf, kill on
        Windows), X22 (bash 5.2.21 goes on after some special-builtin errors,
        against its manual).
      - Items the user agreed to on "Shell Corpus Gaps" (2026-11-03):
        B1 alias and unalias (the parser expands aliases in the lines after a
        top-level definition, as both shells do; the executor keeps the table
        for eval and . text); B2 history, fc, bind, help, compopt, logout and
        set -o history do nothing by default (parser and run() option
        interactive => reject|nop|dash), and the executor looks command names
        up in a list of tables, @Proc::ShellSpawn::CMDS, which other modules can
        extend; B3 jobs, bg, fg, hash, type, times and reading ulimit, with
        dash's texts and statuses, in the new module Proc::ShellSpawn::Builtins;
        P5 and R1 [ and test are the program, and '<' or '>' between two
        operands need the parser option test_lt_gt; Y2 a backslash at the very
        end is a literal \; Y3 tldr's <Enter> in one-line here-documents, and
        lines with keystrokes left out of the measurement; Q1 kill with a SIG
        signal name is rejected (both shells refuse it).
      - Every measured figure in doc/ names the version it was measured on
        (pre-0.01, 0.01, 0.02, early-post-0.02, 0.03; the table is in
        doc/README.md).  The figures of 0.03 were measured again on the release:
        the corpus yardsticks, the work list and the totals over the tests came
        out the same.  Corrected: spec section 8's ShellCheck figures (now as in
        the ceiling document), test-pools.md's size of the per-test record
        (1,066 lines), and the count of samples accepted only thanks to class X
        (258, not 259); test-pools.md's regrouping count is of 0.03 (577 groups,
        checked).
      - doc/checklists/corpus-gaps.html is the page "Shell Corpus Gaps" as
        published (its third version, with the figures of 0.03), and
        doc/large-subset/pages/gaps-items.txt.lede its lede (the lede had been
        edited only in the published page).  t/dash-known.txt no longer lists the
        line of largebattery.txt with an alias as a known difference: since B1 it
        agrees with dash, so a change there now fails t/dash.t.
      - lib/Proc/Spawner/Docs.pod: a map of the Markdown documents in doc/;
        doc/large-subset/large-subset-state.md.html: the same text as the .md,
        with a Markdeep footer (an experiment in how MetaCPAN shows them).
      - Proc::ShellSubset::Large no longer claims the package name
        Proc::ShellSubset for the PAUSE indexer (MetaCPAN showed it as the
        home of Proc::ShellSubset).

0.02  2026-11-02
      - Documentation: POD for Proc::ShellSubset, Proc::ShellSubset::Small and
        Proc::ShellSubset::Large; doc/large-subset/ (the large parser's
        specification, how its corpus coverage is measured, with the scripts in
        doc/large-subset/tools/, and its history); doc/parsers/ (the base
        parser's grammar and the provenance of its corpus tests); three more
        decision pages in doc/checklists/.
      - t/parsers.t also checks that Proc::ShellSubset::Small parses every test
        string as Proc::ShellSubset does, and, with dash and bash in PATH, that
        both shells accept what only the large grammar accepts; the checks that
        need dash and bash are skipped without them (they used to fail).
      - The message for a construct outside the subset found at run time
        (status 248) now names Proc::ShellSubset::Large, not ShellSubsetLarge.
      - README: the aim is matching dash/bash (what both do alike), not dash
        alone; also portable shell scripts; every started program is a child of
        the Perl process.  Proc::ShellSpawn's header says the same.
      - Every module has $VERSION.
      - t/dash.t: each run has its own tmp/ for the battery strings that wrote fixed
        files in /tmp, and ignores the length of process IDs turned into N.
        Test runs at the same time no longer fail each other (probably the
        unexplained failure X20).  The kill -INT $$ strings are marked FLAKY:
        X19 also shows with a subshell.

0.01  unreleased (2026-11-02)
      - First version: Proc::Spawner (posix_spawn with every glibc 2.39 setting, the
        one event loop), Proc::Spawner::Graph (the executable spawn graph),
        Proc::ShellSpawn (runs shell one-liners without fork), and the parsers
        Proc::ShellSubset, Proc::ShellSubset::Small, Proc::ShellSubset::Large.
      - Builds with older glibc (seen failing on 2.36): Makefile.PL probes for each
        posix_spawn extension, and an option the libc lacks follows UNSUPPORTED.
      - No "Hexadecimal number > 0xffffffff" warning from t/spawner.t or $((0x...)).
      - Tests: t/graph.t, t/parsers.t, t/dash.t besides t/spawner.t.
      - Former names: PosixSpawn (now inside Proc::Spawner), ShellSpawn,
        ShellSubset, ShellSubsetSmall, ShellSubsetLarge.
